{
  "id": "proj-wine-rack-wall-unit",
  "slug": "wine-rack-wall-unit",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Wine Rack Wall Unit",
  "summary": "A diamond-lattice rack that holds 24 bottles. Half-lap joints, cut on the table saw.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 6,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Cut the 1x10s into a 30 in top and bottom and two 28 1/2 in sides. Glue and screw them into a box with butt joints and check that the diagonals match.",
    "Make two identical lattices, one for the front and one for the back, from 1x2 slats running at 45 degrees on a 4 1/2 in grid. Lay one lattice out full size on the bench and mark every crossing on both slats.",
    "Cut the half-laps on the table saw with the dado stack raised to 3/4 in (half the 1 1/2 in slat width), using the miter gauge at 90 degrees and a stop block so matching notches line up. Test the fit on scrap first; it should press together by hand.",
    "Glue the laps, then trim the slat ends at 45 degrees to fit inside the box. Glue the front lattice flush with the front edge and the back lattice 1 in in from the back edge, so each bottle rests on both.",
    "Sand to 180 grit and wipe on two coats of Danish oil. Screw the French cleat into two wall studs with 3 in screws and the matching half to the top of the box; a full rack weighs about 90 lb."
  ],
